## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of streaming data structures, heap-based algorithms, and amortized complexity analysis in a real-time processing context.

## Key Concepts
- **Two-heap streaming median:** A max-heap stores the lower half; a min-heap stores the upper half.
- **Rebalancing invariant:** Heap sizes differ by at most one, ensuring the median is accessible in O(1).
- **Time complexity:** O(log n) per element for insertion plus O(log n) for rebalancing.
- **Space complexity:** O(n), proportional to total elements seen.

## Code Example
```javascript
insert(x) {
  if (L.isEmpty() || x <= L.max()) {
    L.insert(x);
  } else {
    R.insert(x);
  }
  rebalance();
}

rebalance() {
  if (L.size() > R.size() + 1) {
    R.insert(L.extractMax());
  } else if (R.size() > L.size() + 1) {
    L.insert(R.extractMin());
  }
}

getMedian() {
  if (L.size() > R.size()) return L.max();
  if (R.size() > L.size()) return R.min();
  return (L.max() + R.min()) / 2;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
New angle on complexity-analysis: maintaining LIS under online batched updates tests block-based design, amortized reasoning, and practical trade-offs beyond typical path/graph problems.

## Key Concepts
- Longest Increasing Subsequence (LIS) dynamics
- sqrt-decomposition / block partitioning
- per-block LIS profiles and cross-block composition
- amortized cost vs full recomputation
- practical optimizations and memory bounds

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple block LIS recompute (tails-based)
function computeBlockLIS(block) {
  const tails = [];
  for (const x of block) {
    let i = 0, j = tails.length;
    while (i < j) {
      const m = (i + j) >> 1;
      if (tails[m] < x) i = m + 1; else j = m;
    }
    tails[i] = x;
  }
  return tails;
}

// Placeholder for merging block profiles into global LIS length
function mergeBlocks(blocks) {
  let tails = [];
  for (const b of blocks) {
    // apply b.profile to current tails (conceptual)
    tails = applyProfile(tails, b.profile);
  }
  return tails.length;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for non-increasing or multi-criteria LIS (e.g., by value and position)?
- What would be the impact of choosing K adaptively based on B and n?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests incremental counting, batch updates, and tight per-event amortized bounds, plus modulo-based pair counting with constant extra space.

## Key Concepts
- Residue class bookkeeping with O(1) per update
- Pair counting with modulo constraints
- Amortized vs worst-case trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
// insert/update logic
function apply(x, D, freq, total, op){
  const r = x % D;
  const idx = (D - r) % D;
  if(op==='insert'){ total += freq[idx]; freq[r]++; }
  else { freq[r]--; total -= freq[idx]; }
  return total;
}
```

