## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of streaming algorithms with bounded memory and error guarantees. Reservoir sampling is a fundamental technique that demonstrates trade-offs between memory, accuracy, and per-element computational cost.

## Key Concepts
- **Reservoir Sampling**: Maintains a uniform random sample of size S from an infinite stream
- **Unbiased Estimator**: (N/S) * sumS has expectation equal to the true sum
- **Relative Error Bound**: Using Chernoff bounds, P(|X - E[X]| > epsilon * E[X]) <= 2 * exp(-S * epsilon^2 / 3), giving S >= (3/epsilon^2) * ln(2/delta)
- **Time Complexity**: O(1) per element (constant-time random number generation and potential replacement)
- **Space Complexity**: O(S) for storing the reservoir

## Derivation
The probability that any given stream element ends up in the final reservoir is S/N. By linearity of expectation, E[sumS] = S/N * (sum of all N values), so E[(N/S) * sumS] = true sum — the estimator is unbiased. The variance of sumS scales as O(N * M^2 / S), and the relative standard deviation is O(sqrt(N/S)), giving the error bounds above.

## Code Example
```javascript
let S = 100;
let N = 0, sumS = 0;
let sample = [];

function process(value) {
  N++;
  if (sample.length < S) {
    sample.push(value);
    sumS += value;
  } else {
    let j = Math.floor(Math.random() * N);
    if (j < S) {
      sumS += value - sample[j];
      sample[j] = value;
    }
  }
}

function estimateSum() {
  return N === 0 ? 0 : (N / S) * sumS;
}
```

## Worked Example
M=100, B=10, S=50. Stream: [5, 12, 7, 23, 8, ...]. After first batch of 5 values (N=5): reservoir = [5,12,7,23,8], sumS = 55, estimate = (5/5)*55 = 55 (exact). After 100 values with sum=2340: reservoir has 50 values summing to ~1170, estimate = (100/50)*1170 = 2340 (close to true). With S=50 and epsilon=0.1, delta ≈ exp(-50*0.01/3) ≈ 0.85 — increase S to 400 for delta=0.01.

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests real-time data tracking with batched updates and sliding window counts. It evaluates the ability to design a data structure that combines efficient timestamp-based aggregation with per-session lookup while managing amortized cleanup.

## Key Concepts

- Batched online updates with timestamp ordering
- Sliding window counts with expiration
- Two-tier data structures (ordered map + hash map)
- Amortized analysis versus naive recalculation

## Code Example

```javascript
class SessionTracker {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.idToTime = new Map();
    this.timeToCount = new Map();
  }

  processBatch(events, currentTime) {
    for (const { sessionId, time, isNew } of events) {
      if (isNew) {
        this.idToTime.set(sessionId, time);
        this.timeToCount.set(time, (this.timeToCount.get(time) || 0) + 1);
      } else {
        const oldTime = this.idToTime.get(sessionId);
        this.timeToCount.set(oldTime, this.timeToCount.get(oldTime) - 1);
        this.idToTime.set(sessionId, time);
        this.timeToCount.set(time, (this.timeToCount.get(time) || 0) + 1);
      }
    }
    this.expireOldSessions(currentTime);
  }

  expireOldSessions(currentTime) {
    const threshold = currentTime - this.W;
    for (const [time, count] of this.timeToCount) {
      if (time < threshold) {
        this.timeToCount.delete(time);
      }
    }
  }

  countActive(currentTime) {
    const threshold = currentTime - this.W;
    let count = 0;
    for (const [time, c] of this.timeToCount) {
      if (time >= threshold) count += c;
    }
    return count;
  }
}
```

## Analysis

**Preprocessing**: O(1) — just initialize empty maps.

**Memory**: O(S) where S is the number of unique timestamps with active sessions; worst case O(N) for N sessions if all have distinct timestamps.

**Update**: Each event is O(log T) for the ordered map insertion/deletion, where T is the number of unique timestamps. With B events per batch, this is O(B log T) amortized.

**Query**: O(T) in the worst case — must scan all timestamps in the window. Using an ordered map with prefix sum augmentation could reduce this to O(log T).

**Comparison to naive recalculation**: Recalculating from scratch after each batch would require O(N) per query, where N is total active sessions. The two-tier structure provides O(1) lookup per session update and O(T) or O(log T) queries — a significant improvement when batches are processed frequently and queries are intermittent.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical thinking on append-heavy data structures, cache-aware trade-offs, and I/O modeling under realistic constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Two-tier indexing
- Amortized cost analysis
- Cache & I/O modeling
- Rebuild strategies and hot/cold separation

## Code Example
```javascript
class TieredIndex {
  constructor(B) { this.B=B; this.t1=new Map(); this.t2=new Map(); }
  append(blockId, offset) { this.t1.set(blockId, offset); /* trigger rebuild when needed */ }
  lookup(blockId) { if (this.t1.has(blockId)) return this.t1.get(blockId); return this.t2.get(blockId); }
  rebuild() { for (let [k,v] of this.t1) this.t2.set(k,v); this.t1.clear(); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would this adapt if blocks could be deleted or updated? 
- How would you empirically validate the cost model with real workloads?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests your ability to design a fixed-memory streaming algorithm for heavy hitter detection—a common requirement in real-world scenarios like analytics dashboards, network monitoring, and recommendation systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Misra-Gries algorithm**: A space-efficient algorithm for finding items that appear more than 1/m of the time
- **Heavy hitters**: Values that appear frequently in a stream (above a threshold)
- **Space-time tradeoffs**: Analyzing memory usage versus per-element update time

## Algorithm Analysis
| Metric | Complexity |
|--------|------------|
| Per-element update | O(1) |
| Per-batch query | O(m) = O(1) since m=2 |
| Memory usage | O(m) = O(2) = O(1) fixed |

## Code Example
```javascript
class MisraGries {
  constructor(m = 2) {
    this.m = m;
    this.counters = new Map();
  }

  add(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      this.counters.set(x, this.counters.get(x) + 1);
    } else if (this.counters.size < this.m) {
      this.counters.set(x, 1);
    } else {
      for (const [key, val] of this.counters) {
        this.counters.set(key, val - 1);
        if (this.counters.get(key) === 0) this.counters.delete(key);
      }
    }
  }

  getHeavyHitters() {
    return Array.from(this.counters.entries())
      .filter(([_, count]) => count > 0)
      .map(([value, count]) => ({ value, count }));
  }
}
```

## Small Example (m=2, stream: 1, 2, 1, 3, 1, 2)
| Element | Counter State | Heavy Hitters |
|---------|---------------|---------------|
| 1 | {1:1} | 1 |
| 2 | {1:1, 2:1} | 1, 2 |
| 1 | {1:2, 2:1} | 1, 2 |
| 3 | {1:1, 2:0} → {1:1} | 1 |
| 1 | {1:2} | 1 |
| 2 | {1:2, 2:1} | 1, 2 |

## Follow-up Questions
- **Sliding windows**: Use a timestamped variant or combine with a separate structure tracking element ages
- **Ties**: Resolve by recency (most recently incremented) or arbitrarily since counts are equal
- **Accuracy**: Misra-Gries guarantees all heavy hitters are found but may include false positives; lowering the threshold (increasing m) reduces false positives at higher memory cost

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of batch processing with dynamic statistics and tight performance guarantees, blending order-statistics trees with buffering for amortization analysis.

## Key Concepts
- Order-statistics trees or multisets with subtree sizes and prefix sums
- Bulk-merge and batch amortization techniques
- Maintaining running sums to compute L1 deviations efficiently

## Code Example
```javascript
class TwoTierMedian {
  constructor() {
    this.committed = new OrderStatisticsTree();
    this.buffer = [];
  }

  insertBatch(batch) {
    this.buffer.push(...batch);
    this.buffer.sort((a, b) => a - b);
    this.committed.bulkMerge(this.buffer);
    this.buffer = [];
  }

  getMedian() {
    return this.committed.findByRank(Math.floor(this.committed.size / 2));
  }

  getSumAbsoluteDeviations() {
    const median = this.getMedian();
    return this.committed.sumAbsoluteDeviations(median);
  }
}
```

## Bulk-Merge and Rebalancing Strategy
Sort the buffer, then merge two sorted sequences into the OST using an in-order traversal. After merging, rebalance the tree (e.g., via treap randomization or AVL-style rotations) to maintain O(log n) operations.

## Amortized Per-Element Cost
Let B be the batch size. Sorting takes O(B log B), bulk-merge takes O(B), and rebalancing adds O(B). Amortized per element: O(log B) per batch element, which is optimal compared to O(log n) per individual insert.

## Memory Bounds
- OST stores all committed elements: O(n)
- Buffer stores one batch: O(B)
- Prefix sums at each node: O(n) additional memory

## Two Optimizations
1. **Lazy Buffering**: Accumulate multiple batches before merging to reduce merge frequency
2. **Hybrid Selection**: Use quickselect on the buffer first to find median bounds before querying the OST

## Why This Is Asked
Tests incremental connectivity intuition, DSU basics, and amortized analysis for real-world batch updates.

## Key Concepts
- Union-Find with path compression and union by rank
- Alpha(n)
- Batch vs online update trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
class DSU {
  constructor(n){
    this.parent = Array.from({length:n}, (_,i)=>i);
    this.rank = new Array(n).fill(0);
    this.components = n;
  }
  find(x){ return this.parent[x]===x? x: this.find(this.parent[x]); }
  union(a,b){ let ra=this.find(a), rb=this.find(b); if(ra===rb) return false; if(this.rank[ra]<this.rank[rb])[ra,rb]=[rb,ra]; this.parent[rb]=ra; if(this.rank[ra]===this.rank[rb]) this.rank[ra]++; this.components--; return true; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for directed graphs and counting weak components?
- What heuristics decide when to trigger a rebuild versus continuing online?
